{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED for the fresh-session orientation repair at exact head\n6ab5d566de9186ce8f29b7034fc07738d59554f1 on\ntask/fresh-session-orientation-review-repair.\n\nReviewed in\n/Users/hughpyle/play/gitseq-worktrees/fresh-session-orientation-review-repair,\nclean before and after. Every measurement was taken with `git rev-parse HEAD`\nin the same command as the check; the head never moved. Main 4db6d105 is an\nancestor; exactly the ten reported paths differ from it; `git diff --check` is\nclean; every commit in 4db6d10..HEAD carries a Rests-On trailer. Gates green on\nthat head: make vet, make build, make test, make race. No UI paths changed, so\nno UI gate. Repository main has since moved to 41c9fc0, which this head does\nnot contain; that is the world moving, not a defect in the head.\n\nI ran 43 mutation runs and 7 adversarial probes. Two blockers.\n\n=======================================================================\nBLOCKER 1. The two reviewed forgeries are not closed. The gate now asks\nthe answerer whether the answerer is honest.\n=======================================================================\n\ncmd/gitseq-mcp/orientation.go:161-172. orientationRosterSource reads\nproof.Decision.Verdict, proof.Statement.Ratified and proof.Statement.Retired\nstraight out of the resident's JSON. Those three are fold products. Nothing in\nthe commit carries them, and the client never looks for the ratify event or the\nsupersede event that would establish them, even though both are on the same\nchain it already walks. Every other field in that function is re-derived\nlocally -- the sequencer signature at its rotated key position, the target\ngenesis, the trailers, the tree, the payload text and body. Only the three\nfields that decide authority are taken on the resident's word.\n\nProved at this head. Each probe is the shipped test, unchanged, except that the\nhostile resident sets the one flag it controls:\n\n  A1  authentic, reachable, sequencer-signed, UNRATIFIED self-grant offered\n      with Statement.Ratified=true\n      -\u003e source: resident_cached, no degraded marker,\n         roles [operator participant ratifier].\n         The honest fold view of the same actor at that moment is\n         [participant].\n\n  A2  a genuinely REVOKED grant offered with Statement.Retired=false\n      -\u003e source: resident_cached, operator and ratifier restored.\n\n  A3  a fold-ineffective self-grant offered with Decision.Verdict=effective\n      -\u003e source: resident_cached, ratifier granted.\n\nWhy the shipped tests pass. TestWhoamiRejectsUnratifiedRosterGrantEvenWhenAuthenticAndReachable\n(cmd/gitseq-mcp/orientation_test.go:143) builds its forged orientation with\nprojectedSource, which copies the honest fold's flags, then asserts at line 158\nthat the fixture really is unratified. The revoked test does the same at line\n187. Both pin that the client rejects an attacker who truthfully reports his\nown illegitimacy. They cannot fail for the case that matters. The guards are\nnot decorative -- deleting the ratification check fails the first test and\ndeleting the retirement check fails the second -- they are simply load-bearing\nfor the honest attacker only.\n\nThis is the round-2 finding one indirection deeper. Round 2 accepted an event\nthe fold had ruled ineffective. Round 3 accepts the claim that the fold ruled\nit effective. As in round 2 the answer still ships as source: resident_cached\nwith no degraded marker, which is what makes a cheap check that answers a\ndifferent question worse than no check.\n\nFour statements in the tree assert what the code has not established, and each\nmust change with the fix:\n  - the artifact statement: \"The root tree rejects ineffective, unratified, and\n    retired roster evidence\"\n  - the ready-for-review report: \"The two reviewed forgeries now fail closed\"\n  - internal/service/orientation.go:10-13: \"The client can therefore reject\n    authentic but ineffective, unratified, or retired roster statements.\"\n  - docs/reference.md: \"effective in the resident fold, ratified when required,\n    and not retired\"\n\nAn orientation that reports something it did not verify is the failure this\nline of work exists to prevent, and the prose now carries the claim further\nthan the code reaches.\n\nThe minimum fix: the resident's claim about fold state cannot be the client's\nevidence of fold state. Either derive ratification and retirement for each\ncited source from the chain the client already walks, or stop labelling the\nanswer verified -- no source: resident_cached and no absent degraded marker for\nanything whose authority rests on a flag the answerer wrote.\n\n=======================================================================\nBLOCKER 2. The port from the spike module to the root module dropped the\nguards that made round 2 approvable, and their checks are now unpinned.\n=======================================================================\n\nAt the previously reviewed head 213d416, spike/cmd/gitseq-mcp/main_test.go\ncarried twelve orientation tests. This head carries eight in\ncmd/gitseq-mcp/orientation_test.go. Gone with no replacement:\n\n  TestWhoamiRejectsMatchingHeadWithIncompatibleCacheIdentity\n  TestWhoamiRejectsUnboundResidentMeaning\n  TestWhoamiRetriesConcurrentFrontierMoveWithoutDegradedFalsePositive\n  TestWhoamiMarksResidentWhoseFrontierNeverMatchesAsDegraded\n  TestWhoamiFallsBackToExplicitLocalAudit\n  TestWhoamiDoesNotWaitIndefinitelyForAStalledResident\n\nMutations that SURVIVED ./cmd/gitseq-mcp/... and ./internal/service/..., each\nreverting one behaviour at the exact head:\n\n  head agreement    `if compatible \u0026\u0026 orientation.Head == localHead` -\u003e\n                    `if compatible`. The resident's frontier no longer has to\n                    equal the local head at all.\n  genesis           drop `orientation.Genesis != workspace.Config.Genesis`. An\n                    orientation for a different workroom is accepted.\n  fingerprint       drop `orientation.Fingerprint != fingerprint`. An\n                    orientation for a different actor is accepted.\n  local depth       drop `orientation.Depth != len(commits)-1`. This is the\n                    round-1 \"depth 31337\" guard that round 2 recorded as now\n                    genuinely locally derived.\n  frontier retry    remove the retry block outright; and, separately, let the\n                    retry accept a head that does not match. Both survive:\n                    local_full_audit_frontier_moved and its suppression of the\n                    degraded marker are entirely untested.\n  stall bound       orientationTimeout 1s -\u003e 1h.\n  degraded privacy  return the full app.Actor instead of publicActor on the\n                    offline_full_audit branch. Round 2's lesser finding that\n                    re-adding key custody on that branch alone is not caught\n                    is still open.\n  reachability      drop `!chain[commit]`. Round 2's \"chain-reachability is\n                    uncovered\" is still open. The new \"source reachability\"\n                    subtest at orientation_test.go:224 names a commit of forty\n                    zeros, which is not an object in the repository at all, so\n                    it dies in the signature lookup. I removed the chain check\n                    and the decision-event binding together and the subtest\n                    still passed, so it never reaches the chain-membership\n                    question. The case reachability exists for -- a commit that\n                    does exist but is not on the chain at the claimed frontier\n                    -- is untested.\n  raw actor         drop `intent.ActorFingerprint(signed.ActorKey) !=\n                    proof.Statement.Actor`. Round 2's actor finding is open.\n  signed target     drop `decoded.Target != git:\u003cformat\u003e:\u003cgenesis\u003e`.\n  trailers          drop `intent.EqualRefs(decoded.RestsOn, trailers)`.\n  signed tree       drop `actualTree != signedTree`, so a commit's tree need no\n                    longer be the tree the actor signed.\n  payload ceiling   drop ValidatePayloadTree; and, separately, raise the\n                    ParseEnvelope ceiling from the configured value to 1\u003c\u003c40.\n                    Both survive.\n  decode order      move the size check after json.Unmarshal. The test is named\n                    ...BeforeDecoding, but nothing pins the ordering.\n  decoded kind      drop the roster-kind check on the decoded payload.\n  role sources      allow a role carried with no sources at all; and,\n                    separately, drop the check that a cited source's own role\n                    matches the role it is claimed to grant -- which also\n                    removes the `ratifier from operator` carve-out round 2\n                    flagged as letting ratifier ride in free.\n  kernel chain      in VerifySequencerCommitAt, drop the requirement that the\n                    walked sequence start at the named genesis and end at the\n                    named commit.\n  kernel rotation   in VerifySequencerCommitAt, stop verifying the rotation\n                    commit's own signature and stop refusing a rotation to the\n                    key already in force.\n\nThe genesis, fingerprint and depth guards were covered at the reviewed head\n213d416: TestWhoamiRejectsMatchingHeadWithIncompatibleCacheIdentity had\nsubtests named \"genesis\" and \"fingerprint\", and TestWhoamiRejectsUnboundResidentMeaning\nhad \"fabricated depth\" setting 31337, alongside fabricated evidence, wrong\nname, wrong role and omitted membership authority. The replacement map at\norientation_test.go:206 keeps only the projection-version case of the first and\nnothing of the second.\n\nGuards that did die under mutation, and are genuinely pinned: the folded\nverdict, retirement, ratification, the sequencer signature at the call site,\nthe projected roster kind, the payload text and body binding, the projection\ncontract version, the redirect refusal, the loopback policy, warm-before-\nlisten, the sequencer key actually advancing across a rotation, and the whole\nevidence check. Round 2's finding about the tautological size fixture IS\nclosed: the test now asserts orientationResponseLimit == 64\u003c\u003c10 against a\nliteral and pads with a literal, so weakening the constant a thousandfold fails\nit.\n\nThe surviving guards are present in the code, not missing. I confirmed by probe\nthat a forged depth of 31337 is still rejected at runtime and returns\noffline_full_audit with degraded true. They are unpinned, and this is the head\nthat would be merged.\n\n=======================================================================\nWhat I checked rather than assumed, and found sound\n=======================================================================\n\n  - whoami omits key custody on both branches at this head: the resident answer\n    and the offline_full_audit answer both carry only name and fingerprint.\n  - unknown fingerprints get 404 with Cache-Control: no-store.\n  - the resident refuses to listen before its fold is warm; deleting the\n    refusal while keeping the warming call fails\n    TestNewWarmsVerifiedStateBeforeExposingOrientation.\n  - the 64 KiB bound is enforced before decoding, and the constant is pinned to\n    a literal.\n  - redirects and non-loopback addresses are refused through the shipped\n    newResidentClient, not an injected one.\n  - a stalled orientation route falls back in about 1.4 seconds.\n  - the sequencer signature is verified at the key position that governed the\n    commit: VerifySequencerCommitAt succeeds either side of a rotation, and\n    neutering the key advance fails\n    TestSequencerKeyRotationAcrossFullAndIncrementalVerification.\n\n=======================================================================\nSmaller notes, not blocking\n=======================================================================\n\n  - A wholly stalled resident still makes whoami unreachable. My probe blocked\n    every route and whoami had not returned after 20 seconds; blocking only\n    /v0/orientation/ falls back in 1.4s. The block is in attend -\u003e\n    announce -\u003e post /v0/presence, which has no timeout. That code is\n    byte-identical on main 4db6d10, so this branch did not introduce it -- but\n    the property the round-2 head demonstrated (\"a listening but hung process\n    cannot make the correct fallback unreachable\") does not hold in the merged\n    adapter, and the paragraph this change added to docs/reference.md promises\n    the fallback without that qualification.\n  - VerifySequencerCommitAt (internal/kernel/kernel.go:772) has one test and it\n    is positive only: it asserts success before, at and after a rotation. There\n    is no case where the function must refuse -- a commit signed by the retired\n    key after a rotation, or one off the first-parent chain. The\n    chain-shape mutation above survived for that reason.\n  - bc80652 \"Bridge orientation repair to live request\" is still an empty\n    commit whose message describes work that is not in its tree; round 2 asked\n    that the message say so. It is now buried under the reviewed head and\n    cannot be reworded without invalidating it. Recording it so the note is not\n    lost, not asking for a rewrite.\n\n=======================================================================\nNot verified\n=======================================================================\n\n  - I did not re-run the depth 10 / depth 100 warm-versus-cold benchmark, so\n    the timing figures in the ready-for-review report are unchecked by me. I\n    did read the benchmark: it builds depth with real signed acts, asserts the\n    achieved depth, and requires resident_cached on the warm path and\n    offline_full_audit on the cold one, so it cannot pass with its subject\n    removed.\n  - I exercised MCP repository selection only through the existing suite, which\n    passes; I wrote no new probe for it.\n  - Mutation runs used ./cmd/gitseq-mcp/... and ./internal/service/... (plus\n    ./internal/kernel/... for the kernel mutations), not the whole tree, so a\n    surviving mutation means unpinned by those suites.\n\nAll scratch worktrees I created have been removed and the reviewed worktree is\nclean at 6ab5d566de9186ce8f29b7034fc07738d59554f1.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:bacaeb2a94594b5e02b35e16372b5d0e112ed30c","head":"6ab5d566de9186ce8f29b7034fc07738d59554f1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}